1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,600
But that closeness often leads to conflict.

2
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,600
Farmers lose crops. Elephants lose their homes.

3
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,600
Sometimes, both sides lose more than that.

4
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:10,737
In Sri Lanka,

5
00:00:10,737 --> 00:00:16,792
human–elephant conflict kills hundreds of elephants and more than a hundred people every year,

6
00:00:16,792 --> 00:00:18,789
often at the edge of a village.

7
00:00:18,789 --> 00:00:21,430
The warning almost always comes too late.

8
00:00:21,830 --> 00:00:25,574
Existing systems are detectors trained for one camera.

9
00:00:25,574 --> 00:00:27,723
They stop at elephant detected.

10
00:00:27,723 --> 00:00:31,606
Nobody tells the village how long it has, or what to do.

11
00:00:31,606 --> 00:00:36,460
GajaMitra goes from video to a warning in the villager's own language.

12
00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:40,911
An officer picks a site and uploads a boundary camera clip.

13
00:00:40,911 --> 00:00:44,000
This site is in test mode: alerts are stored,

14
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,815
and no text message reaches a real phone.

15
00:00:46,815 --> 00:00:51,484
Then the officer draws the village boundary: two points on the line,

16
00:00:51,484 --> 00:00:55,330
and one on the village side. No training data is needed.

17
00:00:55,730 --> 00:01:00,522
Meta's SAM 3.1 tracks every elephant from one text prompt: elephant.

18
00:01:00,522 --> 00:01:05,385
Object IDs stay the same across frames, so we get real tracks: speed,

19
00:01:05,385 --> 00:01:10,037
distance to the boundary, and an arrival time. Plain, tested code,

20
00:01:10,037 --> 00:01:12,856
not an AI, turns that into a risk level.

21
00:01:12,856 --> 00:01:16,662
A herd standing still at the fence is never rated low.

22
00:01:16,662 --> 00:01:19,200
This wait is shortened in the video.

23
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:23,498
Two Muse Spark agents read the frames and the tracker data.

24
00:01:23,498 --> 00:01:26,933
One proposes the alert, and the other challenges it.

25
00:01:26,933 --> 00:01:31,955
Then a deterministic guard sets the severity from what the tracker measured.

26
00:01:31,955 --> 00:01:36,250
The AI proposes, the code decides. The warning is issued at once,

27
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:38,232
in English, Sinhala and Tamil.

28
00:01:38,232 --> 00:01:41,800
The translations still need a native speaker's review.

29
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:46,219
A stronger escalation, danger, waits for a wildlife officer,

30
00:01:46,219 --> 00:01:49,837
who sees the exact text and the frames the agents saw.

31
00:01:49,837 --> 00:01:53,990
The warning has already gone out, so approval never delays it.

32
00:01:53,990 --> 00:01:55,464
And if nobody answers,

33
00:01:55,464 --> 00:01:59,551
the escalation is sent automatically when the timer runs out.

34
00:01:59,551 --> 00:02:02,700
The officer is never a single point of failure.

35
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:08,125
Every alert carries an idempotency key, so a retry never sends twice.

36
00:02:08,125 --> 00:02:12,130
The Spring Boot backend re-checks the guard on its own,

37
00:02:12,130 --> 00:02:15,772
and refuses any alert with no stored tracker risk.

38
00:02:15,772 --> 00:02:17,811
Python never sends anything:

39
00:02:17,811 --> 00:02:20,870
every side effect is an audited tool call.

40
00:02:21,270 --> 00:02:24,823
We also ran labelled clips against the real models.

41
00:02:24,823 --> 00:02:27,401
The first run passed six of thirteen.

42
00:02:27,401 --> 00:02:30,676
Most misses were our own labels and boundaries,

43
00:02:30,676 --> 00:02:33,742
and after correcting them all thirteen pass.

44
00:02:33,742 --> 00:02:38,619
That shows the system follows its rules. It is not an accuracy figure,

45
00:02:38,619 --> 00:02:42,939
and independent labels are next. GajaMitra: a warning in time,

46
00:02:42,939 --> 00:02:45,100
in the villager's own language.

